JTAG on the Raspberry Pi 3B
===========================

	config.txt		enable_jtag_gpio=1   (already set)
	tools/jtag/raspi3b.cfg	OpenOCD for the BCM2837
	tools/jtag/checkstub	build-time check on the trampoline
	tools/jtag/unwedge	clear EDSCR.ERR after openocd dies mid-transaction

WHEN EVERY COMMAND PRINTS "DSCR.ERR=1" HUNDREDS OF TIMES, run
tools/jtag/unwedge.  It writes EDRCR.CSE through the mem-AP with the
core's examine deferred, which is the one way to reach that register
without first succeeding at the thing the bit is preventing, and it
prints EDSCR before and after so you can see the bit go.  If the errors
come back on the next session the core itself is stuck rather than just
the sticky bit, and that needs the power.  There is no JTAG route to the
PM watchdog: AP0 reaches the debug APB only, and mem-APs 1 to 3 answer
every peripheral address with zero, the free-running system timer
included.

WHY.  Two days of bring-up went: build a kernel, write 1.9MB to the
card through a filesystem server that is itself one of the things under
test, read it all back to verify, reboot, hope.  Three to six minutes
when it worked.  Twice it silently installed the wrong bytes -- once a
kernel truncated to 8192, once one whose first 256KB were the previous
kernel's -- and each of those cost an hour and a card carried to
another machine.  Loading over JTAG never touches the card, so that
whole class of failure stops existing.

And it is the instrument this port keeps needing.  The SD DMA write
stall was found by adding a print, rebuilding, installing and
rebooting, once per question, for a day.  The thing that actually
cracked it was capturing the controller's registers at the moment of
the stall -- which with a debugger is a read, not a release cycle.

NEVER OPEN THE SERIAL DEVICE FROM A SCRIPT.  screen owns it.  Type and
read through the session and nothing else:

	SESS=`screen -ls | awk '/ttys/{print $1; exit}'`
	screen -S "$SESS" -X stuff "<command>\r"
	screen -S "$SESS" -X hardcopy -h <file>

Opening /dev/cu.* a second time while screen holds it, and killing
processes that hold it mid-transfer, wedges the adapter.  It carries
on driving the line idle-high -- so the pin measures connected and
rock steady -- and stops transmitting altogether.  The board still
prints and no longer hears anything, which from outside is
indistinguishable from a board that has hung.  The only cure is
unplugging the USB adapter, which needs a person at the desk.

That was worked out the expensive way, over most of a day, chasing a
board that was perfectly healthy the whole time.  If input stops,
reach for JTAG (tools/jtag/where, and read AUX_MU_LSR and AUX_MU_STAT
-- never AUX_MU_IO, which pops the receive FIFO and destroys the
evidence), or drive the board from the host instead by writing
os/inferno/raspi3b/board.cmd.


BOARD.CMD HAS THREE WRITERS AND NO LOCK.  kinstall.sh, netbench and
styxstress.py all write os/inferno/raspi3b/board.cmd, the file is one
command deep, and nothing in it records whose command it holds.  So an
unrelated run of any of them replaces the command another is waiting
for.  The waiting run then sees its token never come back, and that is
indistinguishable from a board that stopped answering -- which is how
it was first read, as "the command poller never answered".

kinstall.sh now detects it: it remembers what it wrote and checks on
every poll that board.cmd still holds it.  If not it exits 3 with the
command it wanted and the command it found, says the step never ran,
and does NOT claim anything about the card -- in the steps after the
card is written, "THE CARD WAS NOT CHANGED" would be false.  It also
leaves board.cmd as the other writer set it, rather than letting its
own exit handler overwrite a run that is still going.

Detection is not serialisation.  Two runs still collide; one of them
now says so accurately instead of blaming the board.  If you need them
not to collide, run them one at a time.

	KINSTALL_SELFTEST=1 CMD=$S/board.cmd OUT=$S/board.out S=$S/s \
		sh tools/jtag/kinstall.sh	# clash: exit 3
	KINSTALL_SELFTEST=2 CMD=$S/board.cmd OUT=$S/board.out S=$S/s \
		sh tools/jtag/kinstall.sh	# no clash: exit 1, timeout

The refusal clears the exit handler before exiting.  Without that it
refused and then overwrote the real board.cmd anyway on the way out,
because idle() writes it -- the same collision this change is about,
committed by the thing that tests for it.  That is not hypothetical: a
refusal run replaced a committed netbench stats command with IDLE here,
and it was restored from HEAD.

Both need no board, no adapter and no card, and mode 2 is the control:
without it a comparison that was always unequal would report a clash on
every step and pass mode 1 for the wrong reason.  The self-test refuses
to run against the real board.cmd, since another session may be
mid-install.


WIRING.  The pins are the 40-pin header's own, and the firmware puts
them in ALT4 when enable_jtag_gpio=1 is set:

	signal	GPIO	header pin
	TRST	22	15
	TMS	27	13
	TCK	25	22
	TDI	26	37
	TDO	24	18
	RTCK	23	16
	GND	-	6, 9, 14, 20, 25, 30, 34, 39

Ground to any of the ground pins, and use a short lead: this is a
2.5MHz-ish signal on jumper wire, and a long ground return is the usual
reason a chain that should enumerate does not.

RUNNING IT.  The adapter is deliberately not named in the config,
because that is the part that depends on what is on the desk.  The one
here is an Olimex ARM-USB-TINY-H, which OpenOCD ships a file for:

	openocd -f interface/ftdi/olimex-arm-usb-tiny-h.cfg \
		-f tools/jtag/raspi3b.cfg

The speed is set inside raspi3b.cfg, at 1MHz, and it has to be: giving
"-c 'adapter speed 1000'" BEFORE the -f does nothing, because
target/bcm2837.cfg sets 4000 when it is read and the last writer wins.
1MHz because the A53s come out of reset slow and this is jumper wire to
a header; raise it once the chain answers.

WHAT AN UNWIRED ADAPTER LOOKS LIKE.  This, and it is worth knowing so
it is not mistaken for a broken config:

	Error: JTAG scan chain interrogation failed: all ones
	Error: bcm2837.cpu: IR capture error; saw 0x0f not 0x01
	Error: Invalid ACK (7) in DAP response

"All ones" is an idle TDO line with nothing driving it.  A chain that
is wired but wrong usually reads all zeros or a plausible-looking but
unexpected id; all ones means the adapter is talking to nobody.  The
first thing to check is that the card in the board has
enable_jtag_gpio=1 in its config.txt -- without it the firmware never
puts GPIO 22-27 into ALT4 and those pins are ordinary inputs.

WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE WORKING.  First session, 20 September 2026:

	Info : JTAG tap: bcm2837.cpu tap/device found: 0x4ba00477
	       (mfg: 0x23b (ARM Ltd), part: 0xba00, ver: 0x4)
	Info : bcm2837.cpu0: hardware has 6 breakpoints, 4 watchpoints
	...cpu1, cpu2, cpu3 the same

and then, against a kernel that was running at the time:

	> halt
	bcm2837.cpu0 halted in AArch64 state, current mode: EL1H
	cpsr: 0x60000345  pc: 0xa770c
	MMU: enabled, D-Cache: enabled, I-Cache: enabled
	> emmcregs
	status     0x01ff0000     interrupt   0x00000000
	irpt_mask  0x017f7137     irpt_en     0x00000000
	control0   0x00000100     control1    0x000e0407
	blksizecnt 0x00000200
	> resume

Those seven registers are the ones the SD DMA stall was diagnosed from.
Getting them before this meant editing the driver to print them,
rebuilding, writing 1.9MB to the card, rebooting, and provoking the
failure again -- once per question, several minutes each, and twice the
install silently wrote the wrong bytes.  Here it is one line against a
machine that is already running, and "resume" puts it back.

Worth noticing in that very first dump: irpt_en is 0.  The driver arms
it only when it is about to sleep.  If this controller gates its DMA
request on the interrupt ENABLE rather than the status, that would
explain a stall with Iwriterdy set in the status and no request being
raised -- a hypothesis that had no way of being tested before, because
testing it means writing that register on a live machine.

INSPECTION.  halt, read, resume against a running kernel is solid, and
is what this was wanted for:

	telnet localhost 4444
	> halt
	> emmcregs
	> resume

RESTART.  Loading a kernel needs more than replacing memory, and the
way the first attempt failed is the reason:

	kstack524904 esr fffb7fb9 pc 00080000 lr 00080264

A kernel entry assumes the machine the firmware hands over.  A halted
running kernel has the MMU on, its page tables live, VBAR pointing at
its own vectors and the TLBs full, so 0x80000 faults on the first
instruction.  jtagrestart therefore enters through a trampoline that
converts one into the other:

	> jtagrestart		load kernel8.img, then the trampoline
	> resume

	> jtagbounce		the trampoline only, no image load
	> resume

	> jtagprobe		which stub is resident at 0x70000

This works end to end.  21 September 2026, against a board that was
dead at the time -- "WILDSP type 1" with every register zero, the case
that used to mean carrying the card to another machine:

	> jtagrestart
	stub at 0x70000, image 'os/inferno/raspi3b/kernel8.img',
	    pc (/64): 0x0000000000070000
	> resume

	Inferno/arm64 raspi3b build 1789977800
	JTAG-STUB 128 bytes at 0x70000
	...
	RASPI3B-OK
	FAT-OK /n/dos/kernel8.img is 1923336 bytes
	SD-FS-CREATE-OK
	ETHER-DHCP-OK ip=192.168.8.105 gw=192.168.8.1
	;

Twenty-eight seconds from command to banner, and a shell at the end of
it.  Both numbers in that banner are the falsifiable part: the card
holds build 1789932392 with a 124-byte stub, and what ran is build
1789977800 with the 128-byte one.  It is the host's kernel, not the
card's.

Entry was confirmed directly as well, with a hardware breakpoint:

	> jtagbounce
	> bp 0x80000 4 hw
	> resume
	bcm2837.cpu0 halted ... due to breakpoint, current mode: EL1H
	> reg pc     0x0000000000080000
	> reg x0     0x000000004a544147

which is the boot ABI below, arriving as specified.

jtagbounce is the one to reach for first.  It replaces no memory, so
0x80000 still holds the text of the kernel that is running, and a
successful resume reboots the board into the kernel already in RAM and
prints its banner.  That separates "the trampoline works" from "the
image I loaded is good" -- one shot at both conflates them, and did.

THE JTAG BOOT ABI.  What l.s's jtagstub guarantees, and all that the
kernel's entry code may assume when entered this way:

	EL		EL1
	MMU		off (SCTLR_EL1.M clear)
	D-cache		off (SCTLR_EL1.C clear)
	I-cache		off (SCTLR_EL1.I clear)
	DAIF		masked (D, A, I, F)
	TLBs		invalidated (VMALLE1)
	TTBR0_EL1	0
	TTBR1_EL1	0
	VBAR_EL1	0
	secondaries	halted by the debugger, not running
	x0		0x4a544147 ("JTAG") -- entered warm
	pc		0x80000

x0 is the part worth keeping.  A cold firmware boot and a warm JTAG
restart differ in what the SoC peripherals are doing: DMA channels may
be active, the eMMC may be mid-transaction, USB, the timers and the
interrupt controller are all as the previous kernel left them, and
none of that is reset by a trampoline that only normalises the CPU.
A kernel that knows which way it was entered can quiesce them; one
that does not will find them warm and be surprised.  The kernel does
not yet act on x0.

And x0 is overloaded: on a cold firmware boot it is the DTB pointer,
and boot1 in l.s stores it into dtbptr unconditionally.  So after a
JTAG restart dtbptr holds 0x4a544147, which is not a device tree and
is not even inside RAM.  Nothing on this board reads it today -- it
boots to a shell with dtbptr set to the magic -- but anything that
later enables fdt code here will chase a pointer into nowhere, and the
symptom will be nothing like "the debugger did it".  boot1 should
treat the JTAG magic as "no DTB" before that happens.

THE TRAMPOLINE MUST BE POSITION-INDEPENDENT, and the first one was
not.  It is assembled at its link address inside the kernel and run
from 0x70000, below the load address so an image cannot overwrite the
code about to enter it.  7a assembles "MOV $0x80000, R1" as a load
from a literal pool, and 7l put that pool 0x2f8c bytes past the end of
the stub; four instructions were such loads.  Copied to 0x70000 they
read 0x72f8c, which is not the constant and not kernel memory.

Nothing in the toolchain says a word about it.  It assembles, it
links, the kernel prints "JTAG-STUB 124 bytes at 0x70000", and the
trampoline is scrap.  $0x1005 three instructions later DOES assemble
inline, because it fits a bare MOVZ and 7a takes that path -- so two
constants a few instructions apart compile two different ways and only
one survives the copy.  MOVZ/MOVK is the only form guaranteed to carry
its operand in the instruction, and tools/jtag/checkstub rejects any
ADR, ADRP or literal load in the stub, and any branch leaving it, when
kernel8.img is built.

WHO INSTALLS THE STUB.  OpenOCD does, from jtagstub.bin, which
checkstub cuts out of the same linked image.  The kernel also copies
it to 0x70000 at boot and says so, but that copy belongs to whichever
kernel is running -- against a board booted from an older card it is
an older stub, and against a kernel that wedged before
jtagstubinstall ran it is not there at all.  A debugger that needs the
program it is debugging to have installed the debugger's own entry
code has the dependency backwards, and it fails in exactly the case
the debugger exists for.  jtagprobe reads what is resident, which is
also the only check there has ever been that the kernel's memmove and
cacheflush put the right bytes there.

MEMORY READS ARE NOT COHERENT WITH THE D-CACHE.  This is the most
important limitation here and it is invisible until it fools you.
OpenOCD reads this target's memory over the bus, not through the
core's L1, so what comes back is RAM -- and a value the kernel wrote
recently is still in the cache, not in RAM.

Measured, because the obvious checks do not show it.  Reading the
kernel's "m" gives 0 on a machine that is running perfectly, which
looks exactly like a serious bug.  Reading "kerndate" gives the right
answer and proves nothing, because kerndate is initialised data that
is never written: its RAM copy and its live value are identical either
way.  splpc read the same three times, which is only weak evidence,
since an idle board can genuinely take the same spl call site three
times running.

The test that settles it is a counter that must move.  mach0.ticks is
incremented by the timer interrupt at 100Hz whatever else is
happening:

	t1 ticks=7011      t2 ticks=7011      t3 ticks=7011

six seconds apart, when it had to have advanced by six hundred.  It is
not zero either -- it is a real value from about seventy seconds into
the boot, which is when that line last reached RAM.  (disbeat is no
good for this: it only counts while the Dis VM is running, and at a
shell prompt the VM is blocked.)

So:

	core registers		live -- they come from the core's own
				register file, not the bus
	device registers	live -- peripheral space is not cached,
				which is why emmcregs works
	breakpoints, stepping	live
	kernel variables	NOT live; you are reading RAM
	loading an image	fine -- RAM is what the trampoline
				makes authoritative

To ask what a kernel variable holds, have the kernel print it.  To ask
what the machine is doing, use the registers: tools/jtag/where, a
breakpoint, the pc.  A hardware breakpoint at the function and a look
at the register holding the pointer answers "what is this pointer" and
a memory read does not.

One consequence worth writing next to the ABI table: load_image writes
land in RAM, while the trampoline's DC CIVAC over the image range
cleans dirty lines back out to RAM -- the wrong direction for bytes
that were just loaded.  The old kernel's data and bss lie inside that
range, so such lines exist.  It demonstrably works, repeatedly and
with the right build number, so this is not a change being proposed on
an argument; it is the hazard to suspect first if a JTAG-loaded kernel
ever misbehaves in a way that looks like corruption at repeatable
addresses.  The fix, if it is ever needed, is to invalidate that range
rather than clean it, because after a load RAM is authoritative and a
dirty line is by definition the previous kernel's.

WHAT IT WILL NOT DO.  It does not reset the machine, on purpose: a
reset hands control back to the VideoCore firmware, which loads
whatever is on the card and undoes the point.  reset_config is
trst_only in any case -- TRST resets the TAP, not the SoC.  And it
does not replace the serial console: the kernel's own prints still go
out the PL011, and that is still the fastest way to see what it thinks
it is doing.

A NOTE ON THE OTHER CORES.  Cores 2 and 3 halt at EL2H, pc 0x7c, with
the MMU off -- the VideoCore firmware's spin table.  They are not in
kernel text and never have been, because this kernel starts no
secondaries; an earlier version of this file said they park at WFE
inside the kernel, which was wrong.  Halting them costs nothing and
means no core is executing while memory is replaced.

AND A WARNING ABOUT load_image.  Loading an image over a running
kernel replaces its data section with that section's boot-time
contents, so every live pointer, list and counter in it becomes a
constant from the past.  The board may keep answering the console
afterwards and be thoroughly corrupt; one such load produced

	TRAP type 0 esr 96000004 far b900080152b00169

whose "faulting address" is two AArch64 instruction encodings, i.e. a
pointer read out of memory that had been overwritten with code.  That
is fine when the next thing to run is the trampoline, which reinitialises
everything.  It is not fine to take a measurement afterwards, and
numbers from such a boot should be thrown away.
